MARKDOWN.append((
    '### 4A.5. Configure Train-Only Feature Selection\n'
    '\n'
    'This configuration block is placed here so selector settings are chosen after the train/test split is defined and right before the selector implementation in `4B`.\n'
))

MARKDOWN.append((
    '### Before You Run: 4A.5. Configure Feature Selection\n'
    '\n'
    '**What this does.** Chooses how the notebook reduces thousands of molecular features to a smaller set using only the training split.\n'
    '\n'
    '**Main choices.** `elasticnetcv` is more thorough but can be slow on large/high-dimensional datasets. `rf_fallback` is usually safer for quick interactive runs. `none` keeps everything and can make later models slower.\n'
    '\n'
    '**Time cost.** This configuration block is instant; the cost happens in 4B.\n'
    '\n'
    '**What to expect.** Settings are stored for the next block.\n'
    '\n'
    '#### Why these defaults?\n'
    '\n'
    '| Option | Default | Benchmark | Status | Why |\n'
    '| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n'
    "| `feature_selector_method` | `elasticnet_cv` | elasticnet_cv | **Benchmark** | The benchmark's selector. Selection is fitted on the training split only. |\n"
    '| `lasso_feature_selection_alpha` | `1.0` |  | **No effect** | Read only by `fixed_lasso`. |\n'
    '| `selector_alpha_grid_min_log10` | `-5` | -5 | **Benchmark** | Same alpha grid as the benchmark. |\n'
    '| `selector_alpha_grid_max_log10` | `-1` | -1 | **Benchmark** | Same alpha grid as the benchmark. |\n'
    '| `selector_alpha_grid_size` | `12` | 12 | **Benchmark** | Same alpha grid as the benchmark. |\n'
    '| `elasticnet_l1_ratio_grid` | `0.3, 0.7` | 0.3, 0.7 | **Benchmark** | Same l1-ratio grid as the benchmark. |\n'
    '| `selector_cv_folds` | `3` | 3 | **Benchmark** | As in the benchmark. The selector took a median of 295 s per dataset with 3 folds on 32 cores; 5 or 10 folds cost proportionally more. |\n'
    '| `lasso_coefficient_threshold` | `1e-10` | 1e-10 | **Benchmark** | As in the benchmark. |\n'
    '| `lasso_max_iter` | `10000` | 10000 | **Benchmark** | As in the benchmark. |\n'
    '| `lasso_coordinate_selection` | `cyclic coordinate updates` |  | **Untested** | scikit-learn default (cyclic). |\n'
    '| `estimate_selector_runtime` | off |  | **No effect** | Prints an estimate only. |\n'
    '| `selector_auto_rf_by_dataset_size` | on | off (A100); on (RTX run) | **Lighter** | Switches to a random-forest selector when ElasticNetCV is predicted to exceed the threshold. On for a laptop or Colab; the A100 run could afford ElasticNetCV everywhere. |\n'
    '| `selector_auto_rf_threshold_seconds` | `7200.0` | 7200 | **Benchmark** | As in the benchmark. |\n'
    "| `selector_auto_rf_log10_slope` | `1.225` | 1.225 | **Benchmark** | Same timing model as the benchmark config. The A100 run's own timings fit a flatter curve (slope 0.77, intercept -0.30 on 32 cores), so this default overestimates time on a fast machine and falls back to random forest sooner, which is the safe direction on Colab. |\n"
    '| `selector_auto_rf_log10_intercept` | `-0.658` | -0.658 | **Benchmark** | See the slope row. |\n'
    "| `max_selected_features` | `0` | 0 (10% of training rows) | **Benchmark** | 0 applies the benchmark rule: keep at most 10% of the training molecules' count, ranked by coefficient size. In the benchmark this kept a median of 94 features (up to 1,076 on hERG-Karim). A fixed number such as 512 overrides the rule. |\n"
    "| `random_forest_selector_trees` | `500` | 400 (fallback) | **Untested** | Used only by the random-forest selector. The benchmark's fallback used 400 trees; the difference was not tested. |\n"
    '| `show_lasso_coefficient_diagnostics` | on |  | **No effect** | Display only. |\n'
    '| `top_lasso_coefficients_to_show` | `30` |  | **No effect** | Display only. |\n'
    '| `enable_feature_selector_cache` | on |  | **No effect** | Caching only. |\n'
    '| `reuse_feature_selector_cache` | on |  | **No effect** | Caching only. |\n'
    '| `selector_cache_run_name` | `AUTO` |  | **No effect** | Cache name. |\n'
))

