MARKDOWN.append((
    '### 4E. Genetic-Algorithm Hyperparameter Tuning\n'
    '\n'
    "The hyperparameter tuning block in this notebook uses ChemML's own **genetic algorithm** implementation: `chemml.optimization.GeneticAlgorithm`.\n"
    '\n'
    'Instead of exhaustively checking every point in a grid, the genetic algorithm proposes candidate hyperparameter combinations, evaluates them with cross-validation on the training set, and evolves toward better configurations over successive generations. That makes it a better match for mixed continuous, integer, and categorical search spaces like the ones used here.\n'
    '\n'
    "By default this block now uses the prepared `4A`/`4B` split, so tuned models are evaluated on the same train/test rows and the same train-only selected features as the conventional models in `4C`. That differs from ChemML's original `ModelScreener`, which screens each representation separately with its own 90/10 random split.\n"
    '\n'
    'The ElasticNet GA search space follows the installed ChemML AutoML implementation: alpha is searched from `1e-4` to `1e-1` on the log scale, and `l1_ratio` is chosen from `0.4, 0.8`. This is a model-tuning step, not a separate feature selector.\n'
    '\n'
    'The defaults below are intentionally moderate for an interactive notebook. If you need a deeper search, increase the population size or number of generations.\n'
    '\n'
    '**Safe beginner defaults:** keep GA enabled only for one or two model families at first, use 3-5 CV folds, and keep generations/population near the defaults. GA tuning is optional; a good `4C` model is enough for the basic workflow.\n'
))

MARKDOWN.append((
    '### Before You Run: 4E. Optional GA Tuning\n'
    '\n'
    '**What this does.** Searches for better model hyperparameters using repeated candidate fits.\n'
    '\n'
    '**Main choices.** Optional. Keep generations, population size, and model list small in an interactive notebook. Tuning is useful after you have a working baseline, but it is not required for a defensible first QSAR model.\n'
    '\n'
    '**Time cost.** 10 minutes to several hours. Runtime grows roughly with models x generations x population x CV folds.\n'
    '\n'
    '**What to expect.** Tuned model rows and checkpoint files. If it is too slow, stop here and continue with 4C results.\n'
    '\n'
    '#### Why these defaults?\n'
    '\n'
    '| Option | Default | Benchmark | Status | Why |\n'
    '| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n'
    '| `ga_cv_folds` | `5` | GA off | **Untested** | Used only if you tick a tune_* box. |\n'
    '| `ga_objective` | `rmse` | not run | **Untested** | RMSE matches how regression winners are ranked elsewhere in this notebook. |\n'
    '| `ga_generations` | `8` | not run | **Untested** | Small search so it finishes in a notebook session. |\n'
    '| `ga_population_size` | `16` | not run | **Untested** | Small search so it finishes in a notebook session. |\n'
    '| `ga_crossover_size` | `12` | not run | **Untested** | Small search so it finishes in a notebook session. |\n'
    '| `ga_mutation_size` | `8` | not run | **Untested** | Small search so it finishes in a notebook session. |\n'
    '| `ga_mutation_probability` | `0.6` | not run | **Untested** | Not tested. |\n'
    '| `ga_early_stopping` | `4` | not run | **Untested** | Stops after 4 generations without improvement. |\n'
    '| `enable_tuned_model_cache` | on |  | **No effect** | Caching only. |\n'
    '| `reuse_tuned_cached_models` | on |  | **No effect** | Caching only. |\n'
    '| `tuned_cache_run_name` | `AUTO` |  | **No effect** | Cache name. |\n'
    '| `previous_ga_run_source` | (empty) |  | **No effect** | Reuses an earlier GA run. |\n'
    '| `upload_previous_ga_run_source` | off |  | **No effect** | Reuses an earlier GA run. |\n'
    '| `tune_elasticnet` | off | off (GA disabled) | **Benchmark** | Off: GA tuning was disabled for all 44 benchmark datasets, and every published result uses untuned models. It multiplies training cost by roughly generations x population x folds (8 x 16 x 5 = 640 fits per model here) for small, unmeasured gains. 4E, 4F and 4G skip when no box is ticked. |\n'
    '| `tune_svr` | off | off (GA disabled) | **Benchmark** | Off: GA tuning was disabled for all 44 benchmark datasets, and every published result uses untuned models. It multiplies training cost by roughly generations x population x folds (8 x 16 x 5 = 640 fits per model here) for small, unmeasured gains. 4E, 4F and 4G skip when no box is ticked. |\n'
    '| `tune_random_forest` | off | off (GA disabled) | **Benchmark** | Off: GA tuning was disabled for all 44 benchmark datasets, and every published result uses untuned models. It multiplies training cost by roughly generations x population x folds (8 x 16 x 5 = 640 fits per model here) for small, unmeasured gains. 4E, 4F and 4G skip when no box is ticked. |\n'
    '| `tune_xgboost` | off | off (GA disabled) | **Benchmark** | Off: GA tuning was disabled for all 44 benchmark datasets, and every published result uses untuned models. It multiplies training cost by roughly generations x population x folds (8 x 16 x 5 = 640 fits per model here) for small, unmeasured gains. 4E, 4F and 4G skip when no box is ticked. If you do tune one model, XGBoost (the best conventional regression model) is the most likely to benefit. |\n'
    '| `tune_catboost` | off | off (GA disabled) | **Benchmark** | Off: GA tuning was disabled for all 44 benchmark datasets, and every published result uses untuned models. It multiplies training cost by roughly generations x population x folds (8 x 16 x 5 = 640 fits per model here) for small, unmeasured gains. 4E, 4F and 4G skip when no box is ticked. |\n'
))

