MARKDOWN.append((
    '## 5. Deep Learning Workflows\n'
    '\n'
    'This section trains the deep-learning workflows in this notebook:\n'
    '\n'
    '- **ChemML MLP** (PyTorch and/or TensorFlow backends)\n'
    '- **TabPFNRegressor** (tabular foundation-model regressor)\n'
    '- **MapLight + GNN** (CatBoost on MapLight classic + pretrained GIN embeddings)\n'
    '\n'
    'In plain language, ChemML takes the same fixed-length molecular feature matrix used by the conventional models and fits a multilayer perceptron:\n'
    '\n'
    '- the input layer receives the molecular features\n'
    '- one or more hidden layers learn nonlinear combinations of those features\n'
    '- the output layer predicts the target value\n'
    '\n'
    'That makes ChemML different from the later **Uni-Mol** workflow:\n'
    '\n'
    '- **ChemML** learns from the 2D feature representation already generated in this notebook\n'
    '- **Uni-Mol** uses a pretrained 3D molecular representation model with conformer generation and a different training stack\n'
    '\n'
    'So ChemML is usually the simpler and lighter deep-learning baseline, while Uni-Mol is the more specialized 3D model family.\n'
))

MARKDOWN.append((
    '### What Each Deep-Learning Model Is\n'
    '\n'
    '**ChemML multilayer perceptron (MLP)** (`5B`). A fully connected feed-forward neural network trained on the same selected molecular features as the conventional models. ChemML is an open-source chemistry and materials machine-learning package from the Hachmann lab; this notebook uses its MLP with either a PyTorch or a TensorFlow backend.\n'
    '> Haghighatlari M, Vishwakarma G, Altarawy D, Subramanian R, Kota BU, Sonpal A, Setlur S, Hachmann J. ChemML: a machine learning and informatics program package for the analysis, mining, and modeling of chemical and materials data. *WIREs Comput Mol Sci.* 2020;10(4):e1458. [doi:10.1002/wcms.1458](https://doi.org/10.1002/wcms.1458) · [code](https://github.com/hachmannlab/chemml)\n'
    '\n'
    '**TabPFN** (`5B`). A *tabular foundation model*: a transformer pretrained on millions of synthetic tabular datasets to perform prediction "in context". It is not trained on your data in the usual sense; it reads the whole training table and the molecules to predict in one forward pass, which is why it is strong on small datasets and has a row limit (`tabpfn_max_train_rows`). The notebook uses TabPFN v2, through the Prior Labs API or the local `tabpfn` package.\n'
    '> Hollmann N, Müller S, Purucker L, Krishnakumar A, Körfer M, Hoo SB, Schirrmeister RT, Hutter F. Accurate predictions on small data with a tabular foundation model. *Nature.* 2025;637:319–326. [doi:10.1038/s41586-024-08328-6](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-08328-6) · [code](https://github.com/PriorLabs/TabPFN)\n'
    '> Original TabPFN: Hollmann N, Müller S, Eggensperger K, Hutter F. TabPFN: a transformer that solves small tabular classification problems in a second. *ICLR* 2023. [arXiv:2207.01848](https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.01848)\n'
    '\n'
    '**MapLight + GNN** (`5B`). Not a neural network trained here: a CatBoost gradient-boosted tree model whose inputs are the MapLight classic features *plus* a learned molecular embedding from a pretrained graph isomorphism network (GIN, the `gin_supervised_masking` checkpoint from DGL-LifeSci). The GIN was pretrained on about 2 million molecules by masking atom attributes and learning to recover them, so its embedding carries structural knowledge from far more chemistry than your dataset. This is the MapLight + GNN entry on the TDC ADMET leaderboard.\n'
    '> Notwell JH, Wood MW. ADMET property prediction through combinations of molecular fingerprints. 2023. [arXiv:2310.00174](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.00174)\n'
    '> GIN pretraining: Hu W, Liu B, Gomes J, Zitnik M, Liang P, Pande V, Leskovec J. Strategies for pre-training graph neural networks. *ICLR* 2020. [arXiv:1905.12265](https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.12265)\n'
    '> GIN architecture: Xu K, Hu W, Leskovec J, Jegelka S. How powerful are graph neural networks? *ICLR* 2019. [arXiv:1810.00826](https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.00826)\n'
    '> Pretrained checkpoint: Li M, Zhou J, Hu J, Fan W, Zhang Y, Gu Y, Karypis G. DGL-LifeSci: an open-source toolkit for deep learning on graphs in life science. *ACS Omega.* 2021;6(41):27233–27238. [doi:10.1021/acsomega.1c04017](https://doi.org/10.1021/acsomega.1c04017)\n'
    '> CatBoost: Prokhorenkova L, Gusev G, Vorobev A, Dorogush AV, Gulin A. CatBoost: unbiased boosting with categorical features. *NeurIPS* 2018. [arXiv:1706.09516](https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.09516)\n'
    '\n'
    "**Tabular CNN** (trained with the conventional models in `4C`). A small one-dimensional convolutional network written for this project: it treats each molecule's feature vector as a 1D signal and applies two convolution blocks (64 filters, kernel size 5), global max pooling and a dense layer. It has no publication of its own. Feature order in a descriptor vector carries no spatial meaning, which is one reason it was the weakest regression model in the benchmark. For background on convolutional networks see LeCun Y, Bengio Y, Hinton G. Deep learning. *Nature.* 2015;521:436–444. [doi:10.1038/nature14539](https://doi.org/10.1038/nature14539)\n"
    '\n'
    'Uni-Mol and Chemprop, the pretrained 3D and graph neural-network models, are introduced in section 6.\n'
))

