MARKDOWN.append((
    '## 7. Ensemble Models\n'
    '\n'
    'An **ensemble** combines predictions from multiple models instead of relying on a single model alone.\n'
    '\n'
    'The workflow in this notebook now runs **three ensemble strategies by default**:\n'
    '\n'
    '1. collect every trained conventional and tuned model, plus Uni-Mol models that saved their internal-fold predictions\n'
    '2. give each member **out-of-fold (OOF)** predictions for the training molecules: conventional models are refitted on\n'
    '   K folds of the training split; Uni-Mol reuses the fold predictions it saved (`cv.data`)\n'
    '3. align their OOF and test predictions on shared molecules\n'
    '4. optionally prune weak or highly redundant members, judged on OOF predictions\n'
    '5. evaluate:\n'
    "   - **OOF Stacking (RidgeCV)**, a meta-model fitted on the members' OOF predictions\n"
    '   - **Weighted average (inverse OOF RMSE)**\n'
    '   - **CFA fusion (score+rank, diversity-aware)**, searched on OOF predictions\n'
    '6. keep all ensemble rows in the results table and choose the strategy for downstream plots by **OOF RMSE**\n'
    '\n'
    'Nothing in this block reads the test set, so the test metrics stay an honest estimate. Choosing members, weights\n'
    'or the final strategy by test error would make the reported ensemble score optimistic. Weighting by in-sample\n'
    'training error would hand the ensemble to whichever model memorises the training set. Deep workflows other than\n'
    'Uni-Mol (ChemML, TabPFN, MapLight + GNN, Chemprop) have no OOF predictions inside a notebook session, so they are\n'
    'not members here. The command-line runner builds OOF predictions for them by refitting per fold.\n'
    '\n'
    'By default, CFA now first keeps only the **best model per workflow family** before the combinatorial fusion search.\n'
    '\n'
    'Why this design:\n'
    '\n'
    '- stacked generalization has shown gains in QSAR and can be strengthened with applicability-domain filtering\n'
    '- simple consensus weighting is not always superior, especially when weak/redundant members are included\n'
    '- CFA-style combinatorial fusion can improve robustness by balancing model performance and diversity, including rank-space fusion when model outputs are heterogeneous\n'
    '- scaffold-aware and leakage-aware validation is critical for realistic molecular generalization\n'
    '\n'
    'References:\n'
    '\n'
    '- Grenet et al. (JCIM, 2019): *Stacked Generalization with Applicability Domain Outperforms Simple QSAR on in Vitro Toxicological Data*  \n'
    '  https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jcim.8b00553\n'
    '- Kwon et al. (BMC Bioinformatics, 2019): *Comprehensive ensemble in QSAR prediction for drug discovery*  \n'
    '  https://bmcbioinformatics.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s12859-019-3135-4\n'
    '- OECD validation principles for (Q)SAR  \n'
    '  https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/topics/policy-sub-issues/assessment-of-chemicals/oecd-principles-for-the-validation-for-regulatory-purposes-of-quantitative-structure-activity-relationship-models.pdf\n'
    '- OECD (Q)SAR Assessment Framework  \n'
    '  https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2023/11/q-sar-assessment-framework-guidance-for-the-regulatory-assessment-of-quantitative-structure-activity-relationship-models-and-predictions_9b064821/d96118f6-en.pdf\n'
    '- MoleculeNet scaffold-split benchmark context  \n'
    '  https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlehtml/2018/sc/c7sc02664a\n'
    '- scikit-learn StackingRegressor / cross-validated meta-training behavior  \n'
    '  https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.ensemble.StackingRegressor.html\n'
    '- CFA repository  \n'
    '  https://github.com/mquazi/cfanalysis\n'
    '- CFA paper  \n'
    '  https://doi.org/10.1186/s13321-023-00799-6\n'
    '\n'
    'In this notebook, the ensemble is evaluated on the **shared molecules predicted by all selected members**. That is especially important when Uni-Mol runs on a filtered subset.\n'
))

MARKDOWN.append((
    '### Before You Run: 7A. Optional Ensemble\n'
    '\n'
    '**What this does.** Combines completed model predictions using leakage-controlled out-of-fold information when available.\n'
    '\n'
    '**Main choices.** Use OOF selection for honest member choice. Ensembles can help, but they are only as good as their member predictions and may refit conventional/tuned members on folds.\n'
    '\n'
    '**Time cost.** Usually 30 seconds to 10 minutes for notebook-scale data; longer if many tuned models need fold predictions.\n'
    '\n'
    '**What to expect.** Ensemble metric rows, member weights, and notes explaining any excluded members.\n'
    '\n'
    '#### Why these defaults?\n'
    '\n'
    '| Option | Default | Benchmark | Status | Why |\n'
    '| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n'
    '| `build_ensemble` | on | on | **Caveat** | Fusion helped classification (beat the best single model on 10/22 datasets, by a median 1.8% when it did) but rarely regression (1/22). The benchmark chose ensemble members using test scores, so these figures are provisional until its out-of-fold rebuild; this notebook already uses out-of-fold predictions. |\n'
    "| `run_oof_stacking_ensemble` | on | on | **Benchmark** | The benchmark's main ensemble; 8 classification wins (provisional, see above). |\n"
    '| `run_weighted_inverse_rmse_ensemble` | on | on | **Benchmark** | As in the benchmark. |\n'
    '| `run_cfa_ensemble` | on | on | **Benchmark** | Combinatorial fusion improved the best model on 3/44 datasets: 2 classification wins, none for regression. Nearly free once base models exist. |\n'
    '| `stacking_cv_folds` | `5` | 5 | **Benchmark** | As in the benchmark. |\n'
    '| `stacking_random_seed` | `42` |  | **No effect** | Seed. |\n'
    '| `ensemble_oof_folds` | `5` | 5 | **Evidence** | Members are weighted on out-of-fold predictions. The benchmark showed that weighting on training predictions gives almost all weight to memorising models such as extra trees; out-of-fold predictions prevent that. |\n'
    '| `exclude_negative_oof_r2_members` | on | on (test R2) | **Evidence** | The benchmark dropped members with negative *test* R2, which leaks the test set. This applies the same filter to out-of-fold R2. |\n'
    '| `drop_highly_correlated_members` | on | on | **Benchmark** | As in the benchmark. |\n'
    "| `max_train_prediction_correlation` | `0.95` | 0.995 | **Untested** | Stricter than the benchmark's 0.995, so near-duplicate members are dropped more often. Not compared. |\n"
    '| `include_conventional` | on | on | **Benchmark** | The benchmark pooled all workflows. |\n'
    '| `include_tuned_conventional` | on | on | **Benchmark** | Used only if 4E ran. |\n'
    '| `include_unimol` | on | on | **Benchmark** | Uni-Mol members use its own saved internal-fold predictions. |\n'
    '| `cfa_best_per_workflow_only` | on | on | **Benchmark** | As in the benchmark. |\n'
    '| `cfa_optimize_metric` | `mae` | mae | **Benchmark** | MAE, as in the benchmark. It sets both the per-workflow member choice and the metric the CFA search minimises. |\n'
    '| `cfa_max_models` | `0` | 0 | **Benchmark** | No limit, as in the benchmark. |\n'
    '| `cfa_max_candidate_subsets` | `100000` | 250000 | **Lighter** | Smaller search than the benchmark, for notebook runtimes. |\n'
))

