MARKDOWN.append((
    '### 9C. Why Applicability Domain Matters\n'
    '\n'
    'A QSAR prediction is most trustworthy when a new molecule is similar, in feature space, to the chemistry used to train the model.  \n'
    'OECD validation guidance treats a **defined applicability domain (AD)** as a core requirement for credible QSAR use, and AD studies repeatedly show that uncertainty rises as chemistry moves away from the training manifold:\n'
    '\n'
    '- molecules far from the training distribution can have deceptively confident-looking predictions\n'
    '- prediction error and uncertainty calibration often degrade as dissimilarity from the training set increases\n'
    '- a practical deployment workflow should flag whether each new prediction appears **in-domain** or **out-of-domain**\n'
    '\n'
    'Sources:\n'
    '\n'
    '- OECD QSAR validation guidance (defined AD principle):  \n'
    '  https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2014/09/guidance-document-on-the-validation-of-quantitative-structure-activity-relationship-q-sar-models_g1ghcc68/9789264085442-en.pdf\n'
    '- Comparative AD-method benchmark (distance/leverage/similarity families):  \n'
    '  https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22534664/\n'
    '- Conformal prediction as an AD/uncertainty framework for cheminformatics:  \n'
    '  https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27088868/\n'
    '- CPSign (2024) open-source conformal toolkit for cheminformatics:  \n'
    '  https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38943219/\n'
    '\n'
    'This notebook uses a **hybrid AD stack**:\n'
    '\n'
    '- MAST-ML / MADML KDE-based AD with calibration-aware rules\n'
    '- kNN-distance AD in standardized feature space\n'
    '- covariance-regularized (Ledoit-Wolf) Mahalanobis AD\n'
    '- a consensus concern label that summarizes agreement across enabled AD methods\n'
    '\n'
    'References for the MAST-ML / MADML component:\n'
    '\n'
    '- Paper: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41524-025-01573-x\n'
    '- MAST-ML repository: https://github.com/uw-cmg/MAST-ML\n'
    '- MADML repository: https://github.com/uw-cmg/materials_application_domain_machine_learning\n'
    '\n'
    "The block below fits these AD guide rails on the notebook's current training feature space and applies them to the latest new-molecule predictions from `9A`.  \n"
    "Because this AD layer operates in the notebook's tabular molecular-feature space, it is most directly aligned with the **conventional**, **tuned conventional**, and **ChemML** workflows.\n"
))

MARKDOWN.append((
    '### 9D. Runtime And Caching Notes\n'
    '\n'
    'The MADML applicability-domain workflow can take several minutes because it performs repeated internal cross-validation and clustered split evaluations.  \n'
    'The added kNN/Mahalanobis diagnostics are typically much lighter and run on the same feature matrix.\n'
    '\n'
    'When you run the block below, the notebook will:\n'
    '\n'
    '- print a **runtime estimate** based on the current dataset size, feature count, and MADML settings\n'
    '- report whether it is using a cached fitted AD model or fitting a new one\n'
    '- report separately when it starts **fitting** and when it starts **applying** the AD model\n'
    '\n'
    '**Safe beginner defaults:** keep `mastml_n_repeats=1`, keep the random forest size near the default, leave caching enabled, and run this only after `9A` predictions look reasonable. For a quick visual check before this heavier AD workflow, use `9B` first.\n'
))

MARKDOWN.append((
    '### Before You Run: 9D. Applicability Domain\n'
    '\n'
    '**What this does.** Estimates whether each new prediction is inside the chemistry space where the model has support.\n'
    '\n'
    '**Main choices.** Keep repeats low for a first run. KNN and Mahalanobis diagnostics are complementary; consensus labels are easier for non-specialists to review.\n'
    '\n'
    '**Time cost.** Often 5-60+ minutes. Repeated validation and random forests dominate runtime.\n'
    '\n'
    '**What to expect.** An in-domain/out-of-domain table and supporting diagnostic columns.\n'
    '\n'
    '#### Why these defaults?\n'
    '\n'
    '| Option | Default | Benchmark | Status | Why |\n'
    '| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n'
    '| `install_mastml_if_missing` | on |  | **No effect** | Installation only. |\n'
    '| `mastml_random_forest_estimators` | `300` |  | **Untested** | The MAST-ML domain model was not part of the benchmark. |\n'
    '| `mastml_n_repeats` | `1` |  | **Untested** | More repeats are slower but steadier. |\n'
    '| `mastml_bins` | `10` |  | **Untested** | Not tested. |\n'
    '| `mastml_kernel` | `epanechnikov` |  | **Untested** | Not tested. |\n'
    '| `mastml_use_custom_bandwidth` | off |  | **Untested** | Off lets MAST-ML pick the bandwidth. |\n'
    '| `mastml_bandwidth` | `1.5` |  | **Untested** | Used only with a custom bandwidth. |\n'
    "| `ad_enable_knn_distance` | on |  | **Caveat** | In the paper's reliability study (reference random forest, 22 TDC datasets) structural domain flags were weak: molecules flagged by kNN Tanimoto distance had only 1.15x the error of in-domain ones, while uncertainty-based flags had 3.30x. Treat a structural out-of-domain flag as a warning, not proof of a bad prediction. |\n"
    '| `ad_knn_neighbors` | `5` |  | **Untested** | Common choice; not tuned. |\n'
    '| `ad_knn_in_domain_quantile` | `0.95` |  | **Untested** | The 5% of training molecules farthest from their neighbours define the boundary. |\n'
    '| `ad_enable_mahalanobis` | on |  | **Caveat** | Descriptor-space distance; same caveat as kNN. |\n'
    '| `ad_mahalanobis_in_domain_quantile` | `0.975` |  | **Untested** | Not tuned. |\n'
    '| `ad_consensus_low_support_ratio` | `0.75` |  | **Untested** | Not tuned. |\n'
    '| `enable_mastml_ad_cache` | on |  | **No effect** | Caching only. |\n'
    '| `reuse_mastml_ad_cache` | on |  | **No effect** | Caching only. |\n'
    '| `mastml_ad_cache_run_name` | `AUTO` |  | **No effect** | Cache name. |\n'
    '| `save_mastml_ad_output` | off |  | **No effect** | Output only. |\n'
    '| `mastml_ad_output_path` | (empty) |  | **No effect** | Output only. |\n'
))

